      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_CONCEPTS_01_STATES_OF_MATTER",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_STATES_OF_MATTER",
          "title": "1. Definition and Three States of Matter",
          "contentMarkdown": "**Matter** is defined as anything that has mass and occupies space. Macroscopically, matter exists in three physical states:\n- **Solids**: possess definite volume and definite shape.\n- **Liquids**: possess definite volume but not definite shape (take the shape of the container).\n- **Gases**: possess neither definite volume nor definite shape (completely occupy the container).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_4DB85A99",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_00C4078E"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "BLK_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_CONCEPTS_02_CLASSIFICATION_OF_MATTER",
          "semanticAnchorId": "ANCHOR_CLASSIFICATION_OF_MATTER",
          "title": "2. Classification of Matter: Pure Substances vs. Mixtures",
          "contentMarkdown": "Matter is classified into **pure substances** and **mixtures**:\n1. **Pure Substances**: have fixed composition. An **element** consists of only one type of particle (atoms or molecules), whereas a **compound** contains two or more elements chemically combined in a fixed ratio.\n2. **Mixtures**: contain two or more substances in any ratio. Classified into **homogeneous mixtures** (uniform composition throughout) and **heterogeneous mixtures** (non-uniform composition).",
          "boundAtomicFactIds": [
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_8F380C3E",
            "AF_GU_JEEMAIN2027_CHE_8ADB0D17_A61A6E29"
          ]
        }
      ],
